<header><p class="enc-kicker">People / group</p><h1 class="enc-title">Lithuanian Riflemen's Union</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1919">1919</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Lithuanian Riflemen's Union is a paramilitary volunteer organisation founded in 1919 by Vladas Putvinskis to support the army during the wars of independence. Between the wars it grew into a mass organisation and took part in the seizure of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/klaipeda">Klaipėda</a> in 1923; it was banned in 1940 under Soviet occupation, and many members joined the anti-Soviet resistance. Re-established in 1989, it now forms part of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lithuania">Lithuania</a>'s national defence.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/lithuanian-riflemens-union.jpg" alt="Lithuanian Riflemen's Union" width="320" height="450" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Lithuanian Riflemen's Union · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Insignia_of_%C5%A0auli%C5%B3_s%C4%85junga.svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Origins</h2><p>The union was established in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kaunas">Kaunas</a> on 27 June 1919 as a shooting section of the Lithuanian Sport Union, while the newly independent state was fighting the Bolshevik Red Army, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/west-russian-volunteer-army">West Russian Volunteer Army</a> and the Polish Armed Forces. Matas Šalčius and other employees of the Press Office had planned a Steel Battalion to protect Kaunas, while a group led by Vladas Putvinskis drafted a statute for a body to support the army across <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lithuania">Lithuania</a>.</p><p>In June 1919 the Press Office invited Putvinskis to its meeting, and he became the union's first commander and main ideologue. The founders included the writers Juozas Tumas-Vaižgantas and Balys Sruoga, the painter Antanas Žmuidzinavičius and the zoologist Tadas Ivanauskas; at first only civilians took part, but soldiers and officers later joined.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>Between the wars the riflemen's units ran <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/theatre">theatres</a>, libraries, sport clubs and bands, the union published the weekly Trimitas, and centres were built in Utena, Tauragė, Alytus and other towns. Laws of 1921, 1924 and 1935 tied it ever more closely to the Ministry of Defence, and in 1935 it was placed directly under the Chief of Defence. It had 33,276 members in 1935 and about 62,000, men and women, by 1940.</p><p>After the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/soviet-union">Soviet Union</a> occupied <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lithuania">Lithuania</a> on 15 June 1940, a Soviet order of 13 July liquidated the union. Many of its most active members were arrested as enemies of the people and sent to gulags, and in June 1941 others were deported, among them the commander, Colonel Pranas Saladžius. Riflemen took part in the uprising of 23 June 1941, formed underground groups such as Laisvės šauliai under the Nazi occupation, and after the Soviets returned in 1944 many joined the Lithuanian partisans. Two of the eight <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/guerrilla-warfare">guerrillas</a> who signed the declaration of the Union of Lithuanian Freedom Fighters on 16 February 1949 were former riflemen.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Legacy</h2><p>Exiles in Chicago declared the union re-established on 7 March 1954 as the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/national-guard-panama">National Guard</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lithuania">Lithuania</a> in Exile. In Lithuania its restoration was announced in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kaunas">Kaunas</a> on 1 June 1989, and the day its activists took an oath at Putvinskis's grave in Kelmė, 20 September 1989, counts as the day of restoration. Riflemen guarded the Parliament and other state buildings during the January Events of 1991, and on 13 January two members of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vilnius">Vilnius</a> regiment, Ignas Šimulionis and Darius Gerbutavičius, were killed. The union works under a law adopted in 2010, and its symbol remains the double cross of the Vytis on a shield.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Lithuanian Riflemen's Union” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The Lithuanian army was founded on 23 November 1918, largely from volunteers, and the <b>Riflemen’s Union</b>, a paramilitary volunteer force, followed in 1919.</blockquote><a href="/empire/republic-of-lithuania">Republic of Lithuania · Military</a></li></ul></section>
